virglrenderer: uniform blocks only read inside their buffers

What: at link time vrend notes each uniform block's GL_UNIFORM_BLOCK_DATA_SIZE
(0 when the GL found the block inactive). A draw or dispatch is skipped and
logged (three lines per context) when an active block has no buffer bound, or
when the buffer from the bound offset is smaller than the block. Every used block
is bound on every draw; before, only "dirty" ones were, but binding points are
numbered across stages and shared with compute, and a program change marked only
the vertex and fragment stages dirty, so a block could read another stage's or
an older buffer. GL buffers (not texture buffers or mapped ones) are allocated
rounded up to 16 bytes: vrend declares a block as an array of vec4, so its data
size is the guest's block size rounded up to 16, while apps size buffers to
their C struct.
Why: the GPU reads a block from its buffer pointer without bounds checks. An
unbound block read address 0 or a stale buffer: the likely cause of the BIF0
page fault at address 0 on 2026-10-04 that panicked macOS (the fuzzer's shaders
declare blocks freely). A guest must not be able to fault the Mac's GPU.
Depends on: virgl-buffer-binding-checks.patch (buffer sizes, range clamping).
Upstream: upstreamable hardening; not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-gpu-ranges.c (draw with an unbound and a too small block
skipped, an exact and a 16-byte rounded one drawn); fuzz-cmd-stream.sh with
gl-oracle.c (checks every active block of every GL draw).
